s":["The square of the expectation","The probability that $X$ is positive","The square root of the variance","The largest possible value of $X$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"34f233ea47d4b0f67214d204","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does variance measure?","options":["The most likely value","The total probability of the sample space","Typical squared distance from the average","The number of possible outcomes only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5fc046b02526d43c4f7c6759","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the computational identity for variance when the moments exist?","options":["$\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]+(E[X])^2$","$\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X]^2-E[X^2]$","$\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]/E[X]$","$\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7364002ac24aed51b3ed2b00","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is standard deviation often easier to interpret than variance?","options":["It is always equal to the mean","It has the same units as the random variable","It ignores spread","It is a probability lying between zero and one"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e53f3b6c27c62f7069308a69","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the variance of a constant random variable?","options":["$0$","$1$","The constant itself","The square of the constant"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f635068e03ac1b9d345a45f2","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"How does adding a constant $b$ affect the variance: $\\operatorname{Var}(X+b)=$?","options":["$\\operatorname{Var}(X)$","$\\operatorname{Var}(X)+b$","$\\operatorname{Var}(X)+b^2$","$b^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b8fb90c9ab834f649b613a7f","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $E[X]=4$ and $E[X^2]=25$, what is $\\operatorname{Var}(X)$?","options":["$9$","$21$","$41$","$625$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"879a18495a72303a34cdc0e0","time_limit":20},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_009","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\operatorname{Var}(aX)$ for a constant $a$?","options":["$a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)$","$a\\operatorname{Var}(X)$","$\\operatorname{Var}(X)$","$a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)+a$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a155fd529edf0fd93528ea36","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If portfolio returns have standard deviation $0.02$, what does that summarize?","options":["The spread or risk of returns on the original return scale","The exact probability of every possible return of the portfolio","The largest possible return only","The number of assets in the portfolio"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f80e2fe3c608e31dcd30c0ae","time_limit":25},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are squared deviations used in the variance formula?","options":["They make all deviations cancel to zero","They remove the need for expectation","They keep the signs of the deviations while giving large deviations more weight","They make deviations nonnegative and emphasize larger deviations"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"58a921fe5af443d5d6d968c4","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must the relevant moments exist for the computational identity?","options":["Both $E[X]$ and $E[X^2]$ must be well defined","The variable must be normal","The support must be finite","The variance must be negative."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aaf4cb8305d01c29996d2842","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can the variance of a random variable be negative?","options":["No; it averages squared deviations, so $\\ge0$","Yes, for variables that are very spread out","Yes, when the mean is negative","Only for continuous random variables"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cdbf556d2ab44fc5dd120bf0","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $E[X]=1$ and $E[X^2]=10$, what is the standard deviation?","options":["$9$","$\\sqrt{10}$","$11$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d36bb03929627e9b7d2cd45f","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=0$, what does that say about $X$?","options":["$X$ is (almost surely) constant","$X$ is uniformly distributed","$X$ has mean $0$ exactly","$X$ must be normally distributed"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"341f9d7c9eda337fdfbe322d","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A random variable takes $0$ and $2$ with probability $1/2$ each. What is its variance?","options":["$2$","$1$","$0$","$4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"95cb89c7fb1a4e64d9e4f18e","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is variance not usually reported in the original units?","options":["It is always dimensionless.","It is measured in squared units because deviations are squared","It has units of probability.","It is measured in inverse units, because it divides by the number of values"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"05f38e9cc4a3e463034a4153","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Increasing variance is sometimes confused with increasing the mean. Which interpretation of larger variance is correct?","options":["It means the total probability is larger","It means the mean must also be larger","Values are more spread out, not a larger mean","It means the variable is always constant"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"04694fe67f1774dca067b4f8","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $X$ is fixed at $-4$, what are $\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ and the standard deviation?","options":["Both are $0$","Variance $16$ and standard deviation $4$","Variance $-4$ and standard deviation $2$","Both are $1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"593337ddd2c304cd53b53b27","time_limit":40},{"id":"pt_batch9_variance_and_standard_deviation_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A student computes $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-E[X]$. What is wrong?","options":["The second moment should be omitted","The mean must be squared in the second term","The formula applies only to probabilities of events","The result must always be divided by two"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"beb5ce4432ed79789d9f2687","time_limit":40}]}}